al draft stages. Source: Updated Language Arts / The Writing Process Book #1.pdf, PDF pages 3–7; teacher rubric page 8.","sourceText":"","questions":[{"id":"brainstorm","label":"Brainstorm ideas about the source prompt (PDF page 3)."},{"id":"topic","label":"Topic sentence (PDF page 4)."},{"id":"detail1","label":"Supporting detail 1 (PDF page 4)."},{"id":"detail2","label":"Supporting detail 2 (PDF page 4)."},{"id":"detail3","label":"Supporting detail 3 (PDF page 4)."},{"id":"closing","label":"Closing sentence (PDF page 4)."},{"id":"draft","label":"First draft: use your plan to write 5–8 sentences (PDF page 5)."},{"id":"revision","label":"Revise and edit using the checklist (PDF page 6)."},{"id":"final","label":"Final draft (PDF page 7)."}],"areas":[1,2,4,5,6,7,8,9],"approval":"approved","active":true,"demo":true,"teacherGuide":"Fictional testing only. Before real use, staff must review the source pages and rubric on PDF page 8. Observe topic, three supporting details, closing, structure, conventions and legibility. Areas 6 and 7 are adult-supported facilitation, not a separate academic curriculum. Record independent / verbal prompt / model / full support. Preserve first submission and revision history.","subject":"Language Arts","metadataUpdatedAt":"2026-10-25T01:32:31.732Z","metadataUpdatedBy":"khaver.meir@gmail.com"},{"id":"LA-WP2-ELECTRICITY","title":"Electricity — writing process","sourceId":"1zujGBiagsLKfwXGQf5VJGhICwDhQhpu2","pages":"3-7","instructions":"Read the source prompt on PDF page 3: describe life without electricity. Follow the supplied brainstorm, organize, first draft, revise/edit and final draft stages. Source: Updated Language Arts / The Writing Process Book #2.pdf, PDF pages 3–7; teacher rubric page 8.","sourceText":"","questions":[{"id":"brainstorm","label":"Brainstorm ideas about the source prompt (PDF page 3)."},{"id":"topic","label":"Topic sentence (PDF page 4)."},{"id":"detail1","label":"Supporting detail 1 (PDF page 4)."},{"id":"detail2","label":"Supporting detail 2 (PDF page 4)."},{"id":"detail3","label":"Supporting detail 3 (PDF page 4)."},{"id":"closing","label":"Closing sentence (PDF page 4)."},{"id":"draft","label":"First draft: use your plan to write 5–8 sentences (PDF page 5)."},{"id":"revision","label":"Revise and edit using the checklist (PDF page 6)."},{"id":"final","label":"Final draft (PDF page 7)."}],"areas":[1,2,4,5,6,8,9],"approval":"approved","active":true,"demo":true,"teacherGuide":"Fictional testing only. Before real use, staff must review the source pages and rubric on PDF page 8. Observe topic, three supporting details, closing, structure, conventions and legibility. Areas 6 and 7 are adult-supported facilitation, not a separate academic curriculum. Record independent / verbal prompt / model / full support. Preserve first submission and revision history.","subject":"Language Arts"},{"id":"DEMO-CONSTRUCTION-V1","title":"To Build Up, First Dig Down: Loads, Soil, and Footings","sourceId":"1v67DY3UvFTbjXNZIRZQA1OrP8r2N11he","pages":"2-18","approval":"approved","active":true,"demo":true,"areas":[1,2,4,5,8,9],"instructions":"Fictional demonstration for Practice Student A (DEMO-01). Work through the field notes first, then answer each mission. Choose an answer when choices appear, and add a typed explanation, a touchscreen or mouse drawing, or both. Save at any time. Submit only when you are ready for fictional teacher review. After feedback, revise and resubmit.\n\nUse the supplied source PDF, Student Workbook Construction Design v1.pdf, for the original full-page illustrations and narrative. Its printed page numbers differ from the PDF page count: PDF pages 2-18 are printed pages 5-21. This digital edition includes source-based field notes and accessible redrawn diagrams; the original PDF remains unchanged and private.\n\nSafety: Do not perform the pencil-drop, ruler, or flour investigation without adult approval and supervision. Use only the light materials listed in the source. Keep the pencil away from faces, avoid flour dust, and clean up safely. If materials are unavailable, predict the results and label them as predictions rather than measurements.","sourceText":"FIELD NOTES · SECTION 1.1 · PDF PAGES 2-10 (PRINTED PAGES 5-13)\n\nNew York City, January 1930. The Empire State Building crew expected to build upward, but first two shifts of 300 workers dug a 55-foot-deep hole until the shovels struck solid rock. The finished building would stand on 210 columns. A column is an upright structural member that carries weight.\n\nGravity is a force: a push or pull. It attracts objects toward Earth. Weight is gravity's pull on an object, so a brick weighs more than a pencil. A building's load includes its own weight and the people, furniture, books, and snow it must support. The load path carries that load from roof to floors, then columns, then foundation, then the ground. The foundation transfers load into the earth; it receives the accumulated load from the structure.\n\nForces can balance. When the ground pushes upward as strongly as a building pushes downward, the building stays still. Solids compress slightly when loaded, and their closely packed particles push back. Loose soil has spaces called gaps. Its grains can shift into those gaps, so it compresses. Solid rock has very few gaps and compresses much less. That is why the Empire State Building crew dug down to rock.\n\nFIELD TEST 1 · SAND. The source compares loose sand with sand packed by pressing it down. With adult approval, the source uses two cups, dry sand, a spoon, an unsharpened pencil, and a ruler; the pencil is released eraser-end down from 12 inches above each cup, then the depth is measured. Predict before testing: which sand will let the pencil sink farther? Record actual measurements only if the supervised test is completed. The model illustrates how gaps and packing affect sinking.\n\nFIELD TEST 2 · RULER AND SPONGE. The source asks students to compare how a sponge squashes and pushes back with a ruler bridging equal-height book stacks as a light paperback is placed at its center and removed. Observe the bend and recovery. A table bends too little to see, yet it also pushes back. Never use a heavy load or force a ruler to snap.\n\nFIELD NOTES · SECTION 1.2 · PDF PAGES 11-18 (PRINTED PAGES 14-21)\n\nWinnipeg, Canada, October 1913. A newly completed grain elevator contained 65 tall concrete bins. Workers filled the bins with wheat. In less than a day one side sank, the other rose, and the building leaned about 27 degrees. The concrete stayed intact: soft clay beneath the elevator failed under the load.\n\nArea is the amount of surface covered. The same 120-pound hiker presses on a small patch in boots and a larger patch in snowshoes. Spreading the same weight over more area reduces pressure. Pressure is the push on each piece of area: divide weight by area. In the source example, 120 lb ÷ 60 sq in = 2 lb/sq in for boots; 120 lb ÷ 240 sq in = 0.5 lb/sq in for snowshoes. The weight stays the same while the pressure becomes one quarter as large.\n\nA footing is a wide, flat concrete block under a column or wall. Like a snowshoe, it spreads a column's load over more ground and lowers pressure. A footing must match the ground: rock can support a smaller footing, firm sand needs a wider one, and soft clay needs the widest. If soil is too soft even for wide footings, engineers may carry the load deeper to stronger ground.\n\nFIELD TEST 3 · FOOTING MODEL. The source compares a sand-filled paper cup set directly on a shallow bed of flour with the same cup set on a roughly 5-inch cardboard square. With adult approval and supervision, wait 10 seconds, then compare the depth of the two prints. Pour flour slowly to avoid dust. The cup's weight stays constant while the supported area changes. Record measurements only if performed; otherwise label a prediction.\n\nDIAGRAM READING GUIDE\n1. Load path: roof → floors → columns → foundation → solid rock.\n2. Force balance: downward gravity/load is matched by an upward push from firm ground; weak pushback lets a structure sink.\n3. Soil model: loose grains have gaps; squeezing moves grains into gaps; solid rock has almost none.\n4. Pressure model: same load ÷ larger area = less pressure.\n5. Footing comparison: a narrow column concentrates load; a wide footing spreads it.\n\nWORDS TO USE: force, gravity, weight, load, load path, column, foundation, balance, soil, gaps, area, pressure, footing, clay.","teacherGuide":"Fictional source-conversion demonstration. Source: Student Workbook Construction Design v1.pdf, exact PDF pages 2-18 (printed pages 5-21). This lesson maps the complete two-section content and all three source experiments. Check understanding from student explanations and evidence, not from one selected option. Expected source-based evidence: first column on rock; gravity causes weight; load path roof/floors/columns/foundation/ground; weak ground pushback causes sinking; loose sand tends to admit deeper sinking; ruler returns toward its unladen shape; clay failed under the Winnipeg elevator while concrete remained intact; snowshoes reduce pressure by increasing area; 100 lb/50 sq in = 2 lb/sq in and 100 lb/200 sq in = 0.5 lb/sq in; a footing spreads a column's load; soft clay calls for the widest footing; cardboard spreads the cup's load. For practical trials, distinguish measured results from predictions. Safety requires adult approval and supervision. The source itself says ice should be at least 4 inches thick before a person walks on it; do not use ice walking as a class activity. Review handwriting or typed explanations with the student. Feedback and requested revisions should address accurate source language, numerical reasoning, and evidence; do not invent grades, diagnosis, or placements.","visuals":["load-path","force-balance","soil-gaps","pressure-area","footing"],"questions":[{"id":"column","label":"MISSION 1 · PDF p. 2 (printed p. 5). Select the safest base for the first column, then explain how the source supports your choice.","choices":["Soft dirt halfway down","Solid rock at the bottom"]},{"id":"gravity","label":"MISSION 2 · PDF p. 3 (printed p. 6). Which statement correctly connects gravity, force, and weight? Explain why the brick feels heavier than a pencil.","choices":["Gravity is a push or pull; weight is gravity's pull on an object","Weight is the object's color; gravity only acts on buildings","Gravity pushes objects upward away from Earth"]},{"id":"loadpath","label":"MISSION 3 · PDF p. 4 (printed p. 7). Trace the load path from the roof to the ground. Which part receives the accumulated load from above, and why?"},{"id":"desk","label":"MISSION 4 · PDF p. 5 (printed p. 8). Name the parts that carry a book's weight from a desk top to the floor. Predict what happens if one desk leg is too weak."},{"id":"balance","label":"MISSION 5 · PDF p. 6 (printed p. 9). While a person sits still on a bucket, what force pushes upward? Choose the best explanation and describe what changes if the supporting ice is too weak.","choices":["The bucket pushes up to balance gravity's downward pull","Gravity stops acting when a person sits","The person floats because the bucket has no weight"]},{"id":"soil","label":"MISSION 6 · PDF pp. 7-8 (printed pp. 10-11). Why can a building sink into soft soil? Use push down, push back, grains, and gaps in your explanation.","choices":["Soil grains can shift into gaps, so soft soil compresses","Soft soil has no particles and cannot be pressed","Rock has more gaps than loose soil"]},{"id":"code","label":"CREW CODE · PDF p. 8 (printed p. 11). Decode 2-1-12-1-14-3-5 using 1=A, 2=B, through 26=Z. What word describes equal, opposite forces? Explain how it applies to firm ground."},{"id":"sandtest","label":"FIELD TEST 1 · PDF p. 9 (printed p. 12). Predict which cup lets the pencil sink farther: loose or packed sand? If an adult-supervised test was completed, record each measured depth and label the units; otherwise label your answer as a prediction. Explain using gaps."},{"id":"rulertest","label":"FIELD TEST 2 · PDF p. 10 (printed p. 13). Describe what the ruler did while the light paperback rested on it and after the book was lifted. How does this model a solid pushing back?"},{"id":"elevator","label":"MISSION 7 · PDF pp. 11-12 (printed pp. 14-15). What failed beneath the Winnipeg grain elevator? Use at least two source details, including the 27-degree lean or the condition of the concrete.","choices":["The concrete walls cracked; the ground stayed firm","Soft clay could not push back against the loaded elevator","The wheat became lighter and lifted the building"]},{"id":"snowshoe","label":"MISSION 8 · PDF pp. 12-13 (printed pp. 15-16). Why does the 120-pound hiker sink less in snowshoes than boots? Explain why the weight is unchanged while pressure changes.","choices":["Snowshoes spread the same weight over more area","Snowshoes reduce the hiker's weight","Boots spread weight over more area than snowshoes"]},{"id":"pressuremath","label":"RUN THE NUMBERS · PDF p. 14 (printed p. 17). A 100-pound crate rests first on 50 square inches, then on 200 square inches. Calculate pressure for each board in lb per square inch. Show your division and explain the change."},{"id":"footing","label":"MISSION 9 · PDF pp. 15-16 (printed pp. 18-19). How is a footing like a snowshoe? A heavy building will stand on soft clay: choose a footing size and explain how area changes pressure.","choices":["Small footing","Wide footing","Widest footing"]},{"id":"cardtest","label":"FIELD TEST 3 · PDF p. 17 (printed p. 20). Compare the cup print in flour with and without the cardboard footing. Record supervised measurements in inches or label them as predictions. Explain what stayed the same and what changed."},{"id":"design","label":"ENGINEER'S CHALLENGE · PDF p. 18 (printed p. 21). Design a safe way to keep a heavy wooden shed from sinking into soft ground. Use footing, area, and pressure. If the ground is extremely soft, what deeper solution does the source describe?"}],"media":[{"page":"PDF p. 2 (printed p. 5)","alt":"The construction site shown in the source, with tall concrete columns prepared to carry a building load.","data":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"},{"page":"PDF p. 16 (printed p. 19)","alt":"Workers pour a broad reinforced concrete foundation, an example of spreading a structure load across a wide area.","data":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"}],"subject":"Engineering"},{"id":"CONSTRUCTION-CLASS-DRAFT","title":"To Build Up, First Dig Down: Loads, Soil, and Footings","sourceId":"1v67DY3UvFTbjXNZIRZQA1OrP8r2N11he","pages":"2-18","approval":"draft","active":true,"demo":true,"areas":[1,2,4,5,8,9],"instructions":"Construction investigation and design workbook. Read the field notes first, then complete each mission. Choose an answer when choices appear, and add a typed explanation, a touchscreen, stylus, or mouse drawing, or both. Save your work as you go. Submit when you are ready for teacher review. After feedback, revise and resubmit.\n\nUse the supplied source PDF, Student Workbook Construction Design v1.pdf, for the original full-page illustrations and narrative. Its printed page numbers differ from the PDF page count: PDF pages 2-18 are printed pages 5-21. This digital edition includes source-based field notes and accessible redrawn diagrams; the original PDF remains unchanged and private.\n\nSafety: Do not perform the pencil-drop, ruler, or flour investigation without adult approval and supervision. Use only the light materials listed in the source. Keep the pencil away from faces, avoid flour dust, and clean up safely. If materials are unavailable, predict the results and label them as predictions rather than measurements.","sourceText":"FIELD NOTES · SECTION 1.1 · PDF PAGES 2-10 (PRINTED PAGES 5-13)\n\nNew York City, January 1930. The Empire State Building crew expected to build upward, but first two shifts of 300 workers dug a 55-foot-