innipeg grain elevator? Use at least two source details, including the 27-degree lean or the condition of the concrete.","choices":["The concrete walls cracked; the ground stayed firm","Soft clay could not push back against the loaded elevator","The wheat became lighter and lifted the building"]},{"id":"snowshoe","label":"MISSION 8 · PDF pp. 12-13 (printed pp. 15-16). Why does the 120-pound hiker sink less in snowshoes than boots? Explain why the weight is unchanged while pressure changes.","choices":["Snowshoes spread the same weight over more area","Snowshoes reduce the hiker's weight","Boots spread weight over more area than snowshoes"]},{"id":"pressuremath","label":"RUN THE NUMBERS · PDF p. 14 (printed p. 17). A 100-pound crate rests first on 50 square inches, then on 200 square inches. Calculate pressure for each board in lb per square inch. Show your division and explain the change."},{"id":"footing","label":"MISSION 9 · PDF pp. 15-16 (printed pp. 18-19). How is a footing like a snowshoe? A heavy building will stand on soft clay: choose a footing size and explain how area changes pressure.","choices":["Small footing","Wide footing","Widest footing"]},{"id":"cardtest","label":"FIELD TEST 3 · PDF p. 17 (printed p. 20). Compare the cup print in flour with and without the cardboard footing. Record supervised measurements in inches or label them as predictions. Explain what stayed the same and what changed."},{"id":"design","label":"ENGINEER'S CHALLENGE · PDF p. 18 (printed p. 21). Design a safe way to keep a heavy wooden shed from sinking into soft ground. Use footing, area, and pressure. If the ground is extremely soft, what deeper solution does the source describe?"}],"media":[{"page":"PDF p. 2 (printed p. 5)","alt":"The construction site shown in the source, with tall concrete columns prepared to carry a building load.","data":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"},{"page":"PDF p. 16 (printed p. 19)","alt":"Workers pour a broad reinforced concrete foundation, an example of spreading a structure load across a wide area.","data":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"}],"subject":"Engineering","metadataUpdatedAt":"2026-10-25T01:32:33.974Z","metadataUpdatedBy":"khaver.meir@gmail.com"},{"id":"lesson-0e38207e-85fb-49e7-8c5f-9cf5f757092b","title":"Pressure and area: pilot","subject":"Reading","sourceId":"1v67DY3UvFTbjXNZIRZQA1OrP8r2N11he","pages":"12-14","instructions":"28 min. Open original pp.15-17. Answer briefly.","sourceText":"Skill: explain cause/effect with a source fact. On p.16, 120 lb on a small area sinks. Snowshoes spread the same weight over more area, so the hiker sinks less. Pressure = weight / area: 120/60=2 and 120/240=0.5 lb/sq in. On p.17 compare 100 lb on 50 and 200 sq in.","media":[],"questions":[{"id":"q1","label":"START: Boots or snowshoes sink farther? Why?","choices":[]},{"id":"q2","label":"MODEL: 120 / 60 = __.","choices":[]},{"id":"q3","label":"GUIDED: 120 / 240 = __. Which is less?","choices":[]},{"id":"q4","label":"READ: Explain cause/effect with area and one p.16 fact.","choices":[]},{"id":"q5","label":"INDEPENDENT: Calculate 100/50 and 100/200.","choices":[]},{"id":"q6","label":"END: Find pressure for 60 lb on 30 sq in, then 120. What changed?","choices":[]},{"id":"q7","label":"TRANSFER: Why can a wide shoe help on soft ground?","choices":[]}],"visuals":[],"areas":[1,2,5,8,9],"approval":"draft","active":false,"version":2,"demo":true,"teacherGuide":"DRAFT; staff approval needed. Source print pp.15-17. Answers: start boots; model 2; guided 0.5; independent 2,0.5; end 2,0.5. Accept sound cause/effect and source detail. Timing 2+4+6+9+4+3=28 min.","sourceVersion":1},{"id":"lesson-2f69e450-15c6-4cbe-9802-0abbea9a5534","title":"Pressure and area: pilot","subject":"Engineering","sourceId":"1v67DY3UvFTbjXNZIRZQA1OrP8r2N11he","pages":"12-14","instructions":"28 min. Open original printed pp.15-17. Answer briefly.","sourceText":"Skill: explain cause/effect with a source fact. On p.16, 120 lb on a small area sinks. Snowshoes spread th